The Input (Incoming Request)
Suppose a user opens Postman and sends a POST request to http://localhost:8085/api/auth/login.

Request Body (JSON):

{
  "email": "  Hasan.Attorney@VeritasVault.com  ",
  "password": "SecurePassword2026!"
}

Spring Boot converts this JSON into your Java DTO: LoginRequest request.

request.getEmail() = "  Hasan.Attorney@VeritasVault.com  "

request.getPassword() = "SecurePassword2026!"

Line 1: Normalizing the Email
Java
String normalizedEmail = request.getEmail().toLowerCase().trim();
.trim() cuts off the leading and trailing spaces.

.toLowerCase() converts all uppercase letters to lowercase.

Resulting Variable:

normalizedEmail is now strictly "hasan.attorney@veritasvault.com".

Why this matters: In PostgreSQL, strings are case-sensitive by default ("Hasan@..." is not equal to "hasan@..."). Without normalization, accidental capitalization or phone auto-correct spaces would cause a login failure.

3. Line 2: Creating the Token Package
Java

new UsernamePasswordAuthenticationToken(normalizedEmail, request.getPassword())
This does not create a JWT.

This is a temporary container created by Spring Security that wraps the two raw credentials:

Principal (username/email): "hasan.attorney@veritasvault.com"

Credentials (raw password): "SecurePassword2026!"

Think of it as putting the email and raw password into an envelope to hand to the security guard.

4. Line 3: Handing Off to authenticationManager.authenticate(...)
Java
Authentication authentication = authenticationManager.authenticate(...);
This single line triggers Spring Security's internal verification chain. Behind the scenes, Spring automatically executes three steps:

Step A: Lookup via MyUserDetailsService
Spring calls MyUserDetailsService.loadUserByUsername("hasan.attorney@veritasvault.com").

Hibernate runs: SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = 'hasan.attorney@veritasvault.com';

If the user doesn't exist, it stops immediately and throws UsernameNotFoundException (which turns into BadCredentialsException).

Step B: Password Verification via BCryptPasswordEncoder
Spring takes the plaintext password "SecurePassword2026!" and compares it against the hashed password stored in the database (e.g., "$2a$10$e8...f2k").

If the password doesn't match, it stops immediately and throws BadCredentialsException (HTTP 401).

Step C: Status and Verification Check via MyUserDetails.isEnabled()
Spring calls the isEnabled() method you wrote in MyUserDetails.java:

Java
return user.getStatus() == UserStatus.ACTIVE && Boolean.TRUE.equals(user.getIsEmailVerified());
Case 1: If isEmailVerified == false, it stops immediately and throws DisabledException ("Account email has not been verified").

Case 2: If status == UserStatus.INACTIVE (soft-deleted), it stops immediately and throws DisabledException ("User is disabled").

5. The Outcome
If any check fails: The method terminates immediately. No further lines of code execute, and GlobalExceptionHandler sends back an HTTP 401 or 400 error.

If all checks pass: authenticationManager returns an Authentication object holding the validated user principal and their assigned roles (ROLE_ATTORNEY), and code execution proceeds to the next line.


